  description="Reconstructive versus obliterative prolapse surgery, and what colpocleisis really involves, explained plainly."
  current="/treatment/"
>
  <div class="narrow">
    <p class="crumb"><a href="/treatment/">Treatment</a> &rsaquo; Understanding surgery</p>
    <h1>Understanding surgery</h1>
    <p>
      If non-surgical options have not given you enough relief, or your prolapse
      is severe, surgery may be on the table. Surgeons take one of two
      fundamentally different approaches, and once you understand the
      difference, every conversation with your surgeon gets clearer.
    </p>

    <h2>Reconstructive surgery, or restoring the anatomy</h2>
    <p>
      Reconstructive procedures put the organs back where they belong and
      rebuild the support underneath them, preserving the full function of the
      vagina, intercourse included. Which procedure you are offered depends on
      which organs have prolapsed. Common ones include anterior repair, also
      called colporrhaphy, for a cystocele; posterior repair for a rectocele;
      and apical suspension procedures such as sacrocolpopexy or sacrospinous
      fixation for uterine or vault prolapse. Most are done through the vagina
      or laparoscopically.
    </p>
    <p>
      This works for most women. It is bigger surgery than the obliterative
      option below, though, and no repair comes with a lifetime guarantee. The
      honest numbers are
      on the <a href="/treatment/recurrence/">recurrence page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Obliterative surgery, or colpocleisis</h2>
    <p>
      Colpocleisis takes a completely different route. Rather than rebuilding
      the support, the surgeon sews the front and back walls of the vagina
      together, closing most of the vaginal canal so the pelvic organs
      physically cannot descend. The name comes from Greek: <em>kolpos</em>,
      meaning hollow, and <em>cleisis</em>, meaning closure.
    </p>

    <h3>Who it is for</h3>
    <p>
      Colpocleisis is usually offered to older women with advanced prolapse who
      are certain they no longer want vaginal intercourse, often women for whom
      a longer reconstructive operation would carry real risk because of age or
      other health conditions. It is shorter and less invasive, typically about
      an hour.
    </p>

    <h3>The trade offs, stated plainly</h3>
    <p>
      After colpocleisis, vaginal intercourse is permanently no longer possible,
      and access to the cervix for Pap tests becomes limited. That is why the
      decision deserves unhurried discussion, sometimes with a partner in the
      room. The vulva, the clitoris, and the external anatomy are not changed.
    </p>

    <div class="panel">
      <h3>What the research shows</h3>
      <p>
        For the right candidate the results are excellent. In recent studies
        roughly 95 percent of women had a successful anatomical result,
        satisfaction ran high, symptom scores improved dramatically, and regret
        was rare, under 7 percent. In one study none of the regret related to
        sexual function at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a woman who has struggled with severe prolapse for years, many in
        our community describe it as life changing relief.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Questions worth asking your surgeon</h2>
    <ul class="spaced">
      <li>Which of my organs have prolapsed, and what stage is each one?</li>
      <li>Which procedure are you recommending for me, and why that one?</li>
      <li>What are my odds of the prolapse returning after this specific procedure?</li>
      <li>What will my recovery restrictions be, and for how long?</li>
      <li>How many of these procedures do you perform in a year?</li>
      <li>If I do nothing for now, what is likely to happen?</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="actions" style="margin-top:2rem;">
      <a class="btn btn--quiet" href="/treatment/recovery/">Next: recovery after surgery</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
